1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,212
This video shows where a record goes when your pipeline can't read it,

2
00:00:06,212 --> 00:00:08,550
and how to tell what went wrong.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,691
You need one route that runs.

4
00:00:10,691 --> 00:00:15,089
This one reads JSON records from a Kafka source, tutorial-in,

5
00:00:15,089 --> 00:00:19,110
and writes them to a Kafka destination, tutorial-out.

6
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,663
Its pipeline parses each record as JSON, makes the message uppercase, and adds a field.

7
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:33,779
To build the same route, follow Your first pipeline in the docs.

8
00:00:33,779 --> 00:00:36,270
Any route of yours works the same way.

9
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,258
Your pipeline reads JSON.

10
00:00:38,258 --> 00:00:41,390
Send it a record that isn't JSON.

11
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:45,227
Then read the newest record in the output topic.

12
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:52,163
It arrived.

13
00:00:52,163 --> 00:00:58,870
The record goes on, and smeltr writes what went wrong into a field, smeltr.exception.

14
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,015
The parse step couldn't read it.

15
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:05,710
The exception's kind is Unparsable, and the message quotes the text.

16
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,590
The original text is still in _raw, so nothing is lost.

17
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,502
In smeltr, open Build, and open the route.

18
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:21,910
Its Exceptions say what happens to each category of exception.

19
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:28,160
A parse exception is tagged: the record goes on, with its exceptions in smeltr.exception.

20
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,110
That's what you just saw.

21
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,346
Math and limits are tagged too.

22
00:01:32,346 --> 00:01:36,030
Encoding problems are ignored, and still counted.

23
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:37,671
Unmatched is different.

24
00:01:37,671 --> 00:01:40,991
A record no destination takes blocks the source.

25
00:01:40,991 --> 00:01:46,550
smeltr doesn't accept the record, so it waits at the source while you fix it.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:53,310
Each answer can be changed: ignore, tag, drop, deliver, or block.

27
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,188
Check on learning.

28
00:01:55,388 --> 00:01:57,290
A record fails to parse.

29
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:00,108
Where does it go by default?

30
00:02:04,508 --> 00:02:09,912
On to its destination, with its exceptions in smeltr.exception.

31
00:02:10,812 --> 00:02:15,479
Next, you see what happens when a record has nowhere to go.
